 the antigen of this one it's not  the right shape so we'd need a different  B lymphocyte to make different  antibodies for a different pathogen now  the last thing we need to know about  this are these things called memory  cells because after we've had this  pathogen and wait for them after we've  had this pathogen enter the body some of  these white blood cells remain in the  blood some of these B lymphocytes remain  in the blood and they now refer to as  memory cells because what they do is  they already have this particular  antibody which is specific for this  particular pathogen so if this  particular pathogen was to come back on  memory cells in the blood can rapidly  release these antibodies which can break  down these pathogens really really  quickly so that secondary immune  response is much bigger and is much  faster than the primary one there's  another video about vaccinations that  goes into this in more detail  so just to recap pathogens as a  scientific term for a microorganism  which can cause you harm on the surface  of the pathogen there is an antigen this  is just a protein structure this is the  thing that our white blood cells called  B lymphocytes recognize they produce  antibodies which are complementary to  the shape of the antigen the antibodies  kind of bind to the to the antigen and  breaks them down and once once that  pathogens been destroyed we have memory  cells which stay in the blood which can  which already have this antibody  produced and could release it rapidly if  the pathogen was to come back thank you
 

